## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about DAGs, timing, and dynamic programming over graphs, a common pattern in build pipelines, data workflows, or task scheduling.

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort to linearize a DAG
- DP on DAG to propagate earliest start times
- In-degree based BFS (Kahn) and cycle detection

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestTimes(n, duration, edges) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const es = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let visited = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    visited++;
    for (const v of g[u]) {
      es[v] = Math.max(es[v], es[u] + duration[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const total = Math.max(...es.map((s, i) => s + duration[i]));
  return { earliestStart: es, totalDuration: total };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to detect cycles and report the cycle path?
- How would you extend to handle parallel resources or varying durations per edge?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests stability, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs between sorting algorithms. It asks for a concrete approach rather than a generic answer.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sorting
- Bottom-up merge sort
- Time/space complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSort(arr){
  const n = arr.length; 
  const aux = new Array(n);
  for (let width = 1; width < n; width *= 2){
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += width * 2){
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, n);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, n);
      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right){
        if (arr[p].score <= arr[q].score) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
        else aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
      for (let t = left; t < right; t++) arr[t] = aux[t];
    }
  }
  return arr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for memory-constrained environments where n is very large?
- Compare stability guarantees and real-time performance between mergesort and quicksort in streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests interval DP and both memoization and tabulation approaches. It asks for precise state definitions, correct recurrence when ends match, and careful base cases. It also probes understanding of time/space trade-offs for cubic-time DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP on substrings
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Interval DP with matching ends

## Code Example

```javascript
function minPalRemoval(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    dp[i][i] = 1;
    for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){
      dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i+1][j];
      for(let k=i+1;k<=j;k++){
        if(s[i] === s[k]){
          const left = (k-1 >= i+1) ? dp[i+1][k-1] : 0;
          const right = (k+1 <= j) ? dp[k+1][j] : 0;
          dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], left + right);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you count distinct removal sequences?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) per row with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests mastery of DAG shortest paths and cross-checks understanding of BFS/DFS-derived ordering vs Dijkstra's heap-based approach.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (DFS or Kahn)
- DAG single-source shortest paths via DP
- Edge relaxation and cycle detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function dagSSSP(n, edges, s){
  const g=[...Array(n)].map(()=>[]);
  const indeg=new Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){g[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++;}
  const q=[]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[]; while(q.length){ const u=q.shift(); topo.push(u); for(const [v] of g[u]){ if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);} }
  const INF=1e15; const dist=new Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){ if(dist[u]===INF) continue; for(const [v,w] of g[u]) dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);} 
  return dist;
}
```

